2026/05/05 06:14:13 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If the input contains a modified noun phrase like "the rescued owl" or "the young fox", do not encode the modifier only inside a constant such as `rescued_owl` or `young_fox`. Create a stable entity constant based on the head noun, e.g. `owl1` or `fox1`, assert its class with facts like `(: s1 (Owl owl1) (STV 1.0 1.0))` and `(: s6 (Fox fox1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, and assert modifiers separately, e.g. `(Rescued owl1)` and `(Young fox1)`. If a sentence has a temporal/event phrase such as "after the storm", also create the event entity and relation, e.g. `(Storm storm1)` and `(AfterStorm owl1 storm1)`. If the clause is hedged by words like "it seems", "apparently", or "probably", lower the STV only for the hedged claim/property, e.g. `(Weak owl1) (STV 0.8 0.6)`, while keeping straightforward type facts like `(Owl owl1)` and unhedged facts like `(Alert fox1)` at `(STV 1.0 1.0)`. When generating a query from context, first resolve the definite description using these class/modifier facts: "the rescued owl" should map to `owl1`, not to a newly invented constant. For "Which animal is alert and eating normally?", include an animal/type constraint from context, e.g. `(: $prf (And (Fox $x) (Alert $x) (EatingNormally $x)) $tv)`, rather than the broader `(: $prf (And (Alert $x) (EatingNormally $x)) $tv)`. For "How does X seem?" questions, prefer an answer-bearing representation if you have added one, such as `(Condition owl1 Weak)` queried as `(: $prf (Condition owl1 $condition) $tv)`; if using unary adjective predicates only, keep the ground query very narrow and reuse the exact context constant, e.g. `(: $prf (Weak owl1) $tv)`. Avoid broad predicate-variable or unconstrained conjunction queries because they are more likely to time out.
