2026/05/05 06:15:06 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating a sentence with one event and multiple semantic roles, especially patterns like “X entertained Y at Z with W,” do not encode everything as one high-arity predicate such as `(Entertained event X Y Z W)`. Instead, reify the event and add small role predicates: `(Entertaining event_1)`, `(Agent event_1 X)`, `(Theme event_1 Y)`, `(At event_1 Z)`, `(With event_1 W)`, plus class facts like `(Violinist X)`, `(Guests Y)`, `(Banquet Z)`, `(Tune W)`, `(LivelyTune W)`. For prepositional attachment cases like “the guests at the banquet,” also consider asserting the direct attachment `(At guests_group_1 banquet_1)` so guest-location questions do not require inference through the event. When generating queries from context, reuse these exact predicates and constants: for “Who did the violinist entertain?” query the theme, e.g. `(: $prf (Theme entertain_event_1 $who) $tv)`; for “Where were the guests?” prefer `(: $prf (At guests_group_1 $where) $tv)` if that direct fact exists, otherwise use an anchored event-location query like `(: $prf (At entertain_event_1 $where) $tv)` rather than a broad unanchored `$event` conjunction; for “What did the violinist use to entertain the guests?” query `(: $prf (With entertain_event_1 $thing) $tv)`. Keep PLN queries in the bare form `(: $prf (Predicate args...) $tv)` with variable proof and TV slots, and avoid long positional predicates or unnecessarily broad compound queries that can time out.
