2026/05/05 06:59:44 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating partitive/measure sentences like 'the chef added a cup of barley and a teaspoon of salt', do not hide the measure inside nested terms such as `(CupOf barley)` or `(TeaspoonOf salt)` inside a broad `AddedTo` predicate. Instead create explicit entity constants and decomposed facts: `(Barley barley1)`, `(Salt salt1)`, `(Cup cup1)`, `(Teaspoon teaspoon1)`, `(Adding add1)`, `(Agent add1 chef1)`, `(Theme add1 barley1)`, `(Theme add1 salt1)`, `(To add1 soup1)`, `(Quantity add1 cup1)`, `(Quantity add1 teaspoon1)`, `(Of cup1 barley1)`, `(Of teaspoon1 salt1)`. Prefer also adding direct shortcut facts such as `(QuantityOf add1 barley1 cup1)` and `(QuantityOf add1 salt1 teaspoon1)` so later amount questions can use one simple query. For questions of the form 'How much barley/salt did the chef add?', reuse the existing constants from context and bind the answer object directly, e.g. `(: $prf (QuantityOf add1 barley1 $amount) $tv)` if that fact exists, or otherwise `(: $prf (Of $amount barley1) $tv)`. Do not ask broad queries like `(: $prf (AddedTo chef soup $amount) $tv)`, because they can return the wrong coordinated item, and do not ask ground yes/no queries like `(: $prf (AddedTo chef soup (TeaspoonOf salt)) $tv)` when the expected answer is the amount. For ordering sentences like 'The barley went in before the carrots', assert both event-level and direct item-level facts, especially `(Before barley1 carrots1)`, then answer 'What went in before the carrots?' with a single selective query such as `(: $prf (Before $what carrots1) $tv)`. Avoid compound `And` queries for these simple lookup questions when a direct fact can be asserted and queried, because compound queries often time out.
