2026/05/05 07:00:17 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When converting a result/consequence sentence like "Dario practiced the violin every evening for a month. As a result, he played the solo confidently at the recital", do not create opaque all-in-one predicates such as `(PracticedViolinEveryEveningForAMonth dario)` or `(PlayedSoloConfidentlyAtRecital dario)`, and do not rely only on an `Implication` from the cause to the result. Instead, reify the two actions as event constants and expose their answer-bearing parts as separate facts: e.g. `(: s3 (Practiced practice1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s4 (Agent practice1 dario) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s5 (Instrument practice1 violin1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s8 (OccursEvery practice1 evening1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s9 (During practice1 month1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s10 (Played play1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s11 (Agent play1 dario) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s12 (Patient play1 solo1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s14 (In play1 recital1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, and `(: s15 (ResultOf play1 practice1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`. For manner, prefer an argument-valued relation like `(: s13 (Manner play1 confidently) (STV 1.0 1.0))` rather than only a unary predicate `(Confidently play1)`, because the query can then bind the answer. For a declarative base sentence, output statements only and no queries. When later asked "How did Dario play the solo at the recital?", reuse the event constant from context and query the direct manner relation: `(: $prf (Manner play1 $how) $tv)`. Do not use a variable predicate head like `(: $prf ($how play1) $tv)`, since that is too broad/invalid and can time out. When later asked "What led to Dario playing the solo confidently?", query the causal relation directly: `(: $prf (ResultOf play1 $cause) $tv)`, relying on the practice-event facts to describe the bound cause. If the event constant is not obvious, use a small constrained `And` to identify it, but prefer direct relations once the relevant event constant is already in context.
