2026/05/05 07:01:02 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: For sentences of the form “Before EVENT, AGENT must ACTION1 or ACTION2,” do not encode `Before` as a standalone premise like `(Before (StormArrives))`, and do not put all meaning only in one compressed predicate such as `(MoveEquipmentIndoors crew equipment)`. Instead, reify the relevant entities and events with stable constants: e.g. assert `(Crew crew1)`, `(Storm storm1)`, `(Arriving storm_arrival1)`, `(Theme storm_arrival1 storm1)`, `(Securing secure_event1)`, `(Agent secure_event1 crew1)`, `(Theme secure_event1 tents1)`, `(MovingIndoors move_event1)`, `(Agent move_event1 crew1)`, `(Theme move_event1 equipment1)`, `(Destination move_event1 indoors1)`. Represent the disjunctive obligation as an `Or` over the two action-event descriptions, and attach `(Before secure_event1 storm_arrival1)` and `(Before move_event1 storm_arrival1)` to the respective branches. For follow-up questions, reuse the exact constants and predicates from context. For “What may the crew move indoors?”, prefer a simple role query such as `(: $prf (Theme move_event1 $what) $tv)` rather than querying a derived disjunction. For “When must the crew secure... or move...?”, query the concrete `Before` atoms, e.g. `(: $prf (Before secure_event1 storm_arrival1) $tv)` and `(: $prf (Before move_event1 storm_arrival1) $tv)`. Avoid broad or introspective queries over entire rules, such as `(: $prf (Implication (Premises $when) ...) $tv)`, and avoid single giant `Or`/`And` queries when smaller atomic queries can retrieve the same answer, because compound queries are prone to timeouts.
