2026/05/05 07:01:37 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating comparative subdeletion sentences like "More X [verb/location] than Y [verb/location]", do not collapse the whole sentence into a single zero-argument predicate such as `(MoreXThanY)`. Instead, create explicit constants for the two compared groups and assert a binary comparison fact, e.g. `(: s13 (MoreNumerous hikers_group_1 tents_group_1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`. Also add lightweight supporting facts for the group types and modifiers/locations, e.g. `(Hikers hikers_group_1)`, `(Tents tents_group_1)`, `(At hikers_group_1 ridge1)`, `(At tents_group_1 campsite1)`, and event-role facts if useful. For follow-up questions, reuse the exact context constants and predicates. If asked "What were there more of than [Y]?", query the first argument of the comparison: `(: $prf (MoreNumerous $what tents_group_1) $tv)`, not the whole ground proposition. If asked whether "fewer X than Y" is true, treat that as the inverse of "more X than Y" and query the positive inverse relation, e.g. `(: $prf (MoreNumerous tents_group_1 hikers_group_1) $tv)`, rather than querying `(Not ...)`. Avoid PLN `Not` for this kind of yes/no comparative question unless an explicit negated fact is actually present, because `Not` is not negation-as-failure and can lead to bad or slow queries.
