2026/05/05 07:23:58 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: For aspect-heavy sentences, reify each verb/action as an event constant and expose roles/aspect/time as separate facts instead of using opaque predicates. If the input says “Maya is stirring the soup while the onions brown,” create facts like `(: s8 (Stirring e1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s9 (Agent e1 maya) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s10 (Patient e1 soup1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, optionally `(: s10b (Ongoing e1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, plus a separate browning event and `(: s13 (While e1 e2) (STV 1.0 1.0))`; do not encode it as `(Stirring maya soup)` or `(While (Stirring maya soup) ...)`. If the input says “has chopped ... already,” make a chopping event with `Agent`, `Patient`, and `Completed`; if it says “used to cook stew every Sunday last winter,” make a cooking event with `UsedTo`, `OccursEvery`, and `During`/`Last` facts. For questions, first inspect the context for the matching event constant and then query the smallest direct answer-bearing relation. For example, when context already has `e3` as Maya’s completed chopping event, prefer `(: $prf (Patient e3 $x) $tv)` over a large query like `(: $prf (And (Chopping $e) (Agent $e maya) (Completed $e) (Patient $e $x)) $tv)`. When context has `e4` as the used-to cooking event, use `(: $prf (During e4 $when) $tv)`. Avoid redundant extra queries, avoid broad direct predicates such as `(: $prf (Stirring maya $what) $tv)`, and avoid flat `And` queries with many conjuncts unless the event constant is genuinely ambiguous.
