2026/05/05 07:46:32 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If the input sentence describes a change of state such as “X reopened/closed/opened/started/stopped at TIME after/before being in another state for DURATION”, represent the state change as one or more event constants with separate role facts, rather than as a flat predicate like `(Reopened x time)` or `(ClosedForYears x 8)`. For example, for “The old theater reopened last spring after being closed for eight years,” add facts like `(: a1 (Theater theater1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a2 (Old theater1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a3 (Reopening reopen_event1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a4 (Theme reopen_event1 theater1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a5 (Spring spring1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a6 (LastSpring spring1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a7 (At reopen_event1 spring1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a8 (Closing close_event1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a9 (Theme close_event1 theater1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a10 (EightYears eight_years1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: a11 (Duration close_event1 eight_years1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, and optionally temporal links like `(Before close_event1 reopen_event1)`. When later asked “What reopened last spring?”, reuse the known event constant and query the role directly, e.g. `(: $prf (Theme reopen_event1 $what) $tv)`, rather than a broad flat query like `(: $prf (Reopened $x last_spring) $tv)`. When asked “When did X reopen?”, query `(: $prf (At reopen_event1 $when) $tv)`. When asked “How long had X been closed?”, query `(: $prf (Duration close_event1 $duration) $tv)`. Prefer the smallest direct role relation with one answer variable; avoid unnecessary `And` queries and avoid raw numeric/time arguments when the answer should be a natural-language phrase such as “last spring” or “eight years.”
