2026/05/05 08:26:10 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When a sentence says multiple named people are "helping each other" with an activity, do not encode it as one high-arity group predicate like `(HelpingEachOtherWithHomework Mark Bob Anna)`. Instead, expand the reciprocal relation into explicit directed help facts for every ordered pair of distinct people. Use stable lowercase constants, e.g. `mark1`, `bob1`, `anna1`, `homework1`. A good representation is either direct facts such as `(: s1 (HelpsWith mark1 bob1 homework1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s2 (HelpsWith mark1 anna1 homework1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, etc., or event-role facts with `Helping`, `Agent`, `Recipient`, and `Theme`. For later questions like "Does Mark help Anna with homework?", query the directed relation with Mark as helper/agent and Anna as recipient, e.g. `(: $prf (HelpsWith mark1 anna1 homework1) $tv)` if direct facts exist. For "Who is providing help with homework?", query the helper/agent variable constrained by the homework theme, e.g. `(: $prf (HelpsWith $who $recipient homework1) $tv)`, and rely on answer postprocessing to deduplicate Mark/Bob/Anna. Avoid broad or ambiguous queries that bind only the first argument of a group predicate, because they miss other providers. Also avoid large compound `And` queries when a simple direct predicate can be asserted and queried, since compound event-role queries may time out.
