2026/05/05 08:45:28 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating a sentence with coordinated actions like “After practice, the goalkeeper stretched carefully and drank a bottle of water,” do not collapse each action into opaque predicates such as `(StretchedCarefully goalkeeper1)` or `(DrankWater goalkeeper1)`. Instead, create separate event constants for each conjunct and attach roles/modifiers with small predicates: e.g. assert `(Practice practice1)`, `(Goalkeeper goalkeeper1)`, `(Stretching stretch_event1)`, `(Agent stretch_event1 goalkeeper1)`, `(Carefully stretch_event1)`, `(After stretch_event1 practice1)`, `(Drinking drink_event1)`, `(Agent drink_event1 goalkeeper1)`, `(Theme drink_event1 bottle1)`, `(Bottle bottle1)`, `(Water water1)`, `(Contains bottle1 water1)`, and `(After drink_event1 practice1)`. When later generating queries from this context, reuse the exact event constants and predicates. For “How did the goalkeeper stretch?”, query the manner fact directly, e.g. `(: $prf (Carefully stretch_event1) $tv)`, rather than querying a collapsed action predicate. For “What did the goalkeeper drink?”, query the theme of the known drinking event, e.g. `(: $prf (Theme drink_event1 $what) $tv)`. For “What did the goalkeeper do after practice?”, avoid one broad compound query over `$event` if the relevant events are already known, because it can time out; prefer small anchored queries for the known post-practice events, such as checking `(After stretch_event1 practice1)` and `(After drink_event1 practice1)` and/or querying their action class facts separately. In general, represent conjunction by multiple reified events sharing the same agent/time relation, and generate the smallest direct query needed to bind the requested answer.
