2026/05/05 08:46:20 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When a sentence says a count is bounded by language like “fewer than N”, “less than N”, “more than N”, “at least N”, or “no more than N”, do not encode the count as a `NatDist`/`FloatDist` enumerating possible values and do not invent probability weights. Instead, create a symbolic count/value constant and a separate comparison fact. For example, for “Fewer than ten seats remain open on the afternoon train to Harbor City,” use facts like `(: s5 (OpenSeatsCount train1 open_count1) (STV 1.0 1.0))` and `(: s6 (LessThan open_count1 10) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, plus ordinary train/destination facts. Avoid forms like `(OpenSeatCount train1 (NatDist ((0 0.1) ...)))`, because they are fragile and can fail parsing. For follow-up “How many...” questions about such bounded counts, reuse the count constant from context and query the comparison that carries the answer, e.g. `(: $prf (LessThan open_count1 $limit) $tv)` or a small two-conjunct query tying it to the train: `(: $prf (And (OpenSeatsCount train1 $count) (LessThan $count $limit)) $tv)`. Do not only query an opaque count object if the actual answer is the inequality phrase. For yes/no questions like “Do ten seats remain open?” when context says fewer than ten, query the known comparison fact such as `(: $prf (LessThan open_count1 10) $tv)` rather than trying to derive a negative answer with `GreaterThan` over a distribution or with absence-based negation. For event/time questions, once the event constant is established, query the direct role relation, e.g. `(: $prf (At check1 $when) $tv)`, and keep the query small.
