2026/05/05 09:06:52 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If the input declarative sentences contain topic-comment/fronted objects such as “About the hiking trail, the rangers closed it because of fallen trees” or “The picnic shelter, visitors can still use on weekends”, resolve the fronted topic/pronoun and represent the situation with reified events plus generic roles, not with one-off predicates that bake roles or modifiers into predicate names. For example, create entity/modifier facts like `(: s1 (Trail trail1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s2 (Hiking trail1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s3 (Rangers rangers1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s4 (Trees trees1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s5 (Fallen trees1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, then event facts like `(: s6 (Closing close_event1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s7 (Agent close_event1 rangers1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s8 (Theme close_event1 trail1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s9 (BecauseOf close_event1 trees1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`. For the picnic sentence, similarly use `Shelter`, `Picnic`, `Visitors`, `Weekend`, `Using`, `Agent`, `Theme`, and `On`, e.g. `(: s17 (On use_event1 weekend1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`. Avoid representations like `(ClosedBy rangers hiking_trail)`, `(ClosedBecause hiking_trail fallen_trees)`, or `(CanUseOnWeekends visitors picnic_shelter)` when the sentence contains events, causes, themes, or times, because they make later WH queries less explicit. If the input is a question and context already contains these event-role facts, add no new statements and reuse the existing event constants in narrow queries: for “What did the rangers close?” query `(: $prf (Theme close_event1 $x) $tv)`; for “Why did the rangers close the hiking trail?” query the cause of the known closing event, preferably `(: $prf (BecauseOf close_event1 $x) $tv)` or, if the modifier is needed, `(: $prf (And (BecauseOf close_event1 $x) (Fallen $x)) $tv)`; for “When can visitors still use the picnic shelter?” query `(: $prf (On use_event1 $when) $tv)`. Keep all query syntax in bare PLN form with variable proof and TV slots, and keep queries anchored to known event constants rather than broad unanchored event variables to reduce timeouts.
