2026/05/05 10:13:02 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating a sentence comparing travel times, such as “the express train reached the coast in 3 hours, but the regional bus took 5 hours to reach the same place,” do not rely only on flat facts like `(TravelTime express_train coast (PointMass 3.0))` and then ask broad comparison queries. Instead, create stable event/entity constants and role facts: `(Train express_train1)`, `(Express express_train1)`, `(Bus regional_bus1)`, `(Regional regional_bus1)`, `(Coast coast1)`, `(Reached train_reach1)`, `(Agent train_reach1 express_train1)`, `(Patient train_reach1 coast1)`, `(Duration train_reach1 (PointMass 3.0))`, `(Reached bus_reach1)`, `(Agent bus_reach1 regional_bus1)`, `(Patient bus_reach1 coast1)`, `(Duration bus_reach1 (PointMass 5.0))`. For comparative questions like “Which vehicle took less time?” avoid queries of the form `(: $prf (And (TravelTime $vehicle ... $time1) (TravelTime $other ... $time2) (GreaterThan $time2 $time1)) $tv)`, because broad `And` + `GreaterThan` queries can time out. Prefer adding a tightly anchored derived comparison statement or rule, e.g. a fact/rule for `(TookLessTimeToReachCoast express_train1)` or `(TookLongerThan regional_bus1 express_train1)`, then query the small predicate: `(: $prf (TookLessTimeToReachCoast $vehicle) $tv)` or for a yes/no question `(: $prf (TookLongerThan regional_bus1 express_train1) $tv)`. For “How long did the regional bus take?”, do not compare anything; just query the known event duration directly: `(: $prf (Duration bus_reach1 $d) $tv)`. In short: model the two events explicitly, anchor questions to the known event constants, and keep runtime queries small; put comparison logic into precomputed/derived facts or tightly anchored rules rather than broad variable-heavy query conjunctions.
