2026/05/05 10:37:24 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If the input sentence describes an event relation such as people helping each other with homework, do not encode it as a flat role-baking predicate like `(: s1 (Helping mark anna homework) ...)`. Instead, create explicit entity facts, e.g. `(Person mark1)`, `(Person bob1)`, `(Person anna1)`, `(Homework homework1)`, then create one event constant per directed helping relation with role facts: `(Helping help_event2)`, `(Agent help_event2 mark1)`, `(Beneficiary help_event2 anna1)`, `(Theme help_event2 homework1)`. For reciprocal phrases like “Mark, Bob and Anna are helping each other”, expand to all directed pairs except self-pairs: Mark→Bob, Mark→Anna, Bob→Mark, Bob→Anna, Anna→Mark, Anna→Bob. When later receiving a question, add no new statements; reuse the constants and event IDs already in context. For “Does Mark help Anna with homework?”, identify the existing event with Agent=mark1, Beneficiary=anna1, Theme=homework1, then prefer very narrow anchored queries. Avoid a single long query like `(And (Helping help_event2) (Agent ...) (Beneficiary ...) (Theme ...))` if it may time out; use either the most decisive anchored ground role query or several small ground queries such as `(: $prf (Agent help_event2 mark1) $tv)`, `(: $prf (Beneficiary help_event2 anna1) $tv)`, and `(: $prf (Theme help_event2 homework1) $tv)`. For “Who is providing help with homework?”, query the `Agent` role of helping events whose theme is homework; keep the conjunction minimal, e.g. prefer `(: $prf (And (Theme $e homework1) (Agent $e $who)) $tv)` over a broader three- or four-clause `And`. In general, make event roles explicit in statements, but keep queries short, anchored, and limited to the constraints needed to answer the question.
