2026/05/05 11:04:24 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating a coordinated elliptical schedule sentence like “The north ferry leaves at six, and the south ferry at eight,” treat the second clause as having the omitted verb “leaves” and create two separate reified leaving events. Prefer facts like `(: s1 (Ferry north_ferry1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s2 (North north_ferry1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s3 (Time six1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s4 (Leaving leave_event1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s5 (Agent leave_event1 north_ferry1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s6 (At leave_event1 six1) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, and similarly for `south_ferry1`, `eight1`, and `leave_event2`. Avoid collapsing this into a flat predicate such as `(LeavesAt north_ferry 6)` / `(LeavesAt south_ferry 8)`, and avoid bare numeric time constants when the natural-language answer is a named time like “six” or “eight.” For follow-up questions, add no new statements; instead, reuse the exact constants and predicates from context. For “When does the north ferry leave?”, query the anchored time relation: `(: $prf (At leave_event1 $when) $tv)`. For “Which ferry leaves at eight?”, identify the event already at `eight1` and query its agent: `(: $prf (Agent leave_event2 $x) $tv)`. For “Does the south ferry leave at six?”, query the known south-ferry leaving event against `six1`: `(: $prf (At leave_event2 six1) $tv)`. Keep queries small and anchored to known event constants rather than inventing a new flat predicate or using broad unanchored `And` queries.
