2026/05/05 11:22:48 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When the declarative input describes quantities earned/received/scored in multiple parts plus an overall aggregate, e.g. an entity earned N points in one round and M points in another, and altogether earned T, represent the entity and parts explicitly and use generic event-role facts. Create stable constants such as robotics_team1, morning_round1, afternoon_round1, earn_event1, earn_event2, earn_event3; assert class/modifier facts like (Team robotics_team1), (Robotics robotics_team1), (Round afternoon_round1), (Afternoon afternoon_round1); assert each earning as (Earned earn_eventK), (Agent earn_eventK robotics_team1), optional (At earn_eventK round_const), and (Points earn_eventK value). Also assert the stated aggregate directly as (Points robotics_team1 T). Avoid flat one-off predicates such as (EarnedPoints team round n) or (TotalPoints team n) when the sentence has events, rounds, and an aggregate, because later questions work better with the shared Points predicate and anchored event constants. When translating questions against such context, add no new statements and reuse exact constants/predicates from context: for an altogether how-many question, query only the direct aggregate fact, e.g. (: $prf (Points robotics_team1 $howmany) $tv); for a specific round, first identify the existing event with the relevant At fact and query just its points, e.g. (: $prf (Points earn_event2 $x) $tv); for a more-than-threshold yes/no question, bind the existing total and compare it, e.g. (: $prf (And (Points robotics_team1 $points) (GreaterThan $points 40)) $tv). Do not introduce PointMass or a new TotalPoints predicate in the question query, and avoid broad multi-clause conjunctions when a known event or entity constant can anchor the lookup.
